/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f2c955db89ff2206ed531f593a149661417e293b1cffba8bae7ce025cdce38d"} {"question" "q-90793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+100{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+100) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+100=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation has pure imaginary roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+100 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-100 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 10\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP=5.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=20.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-10P\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x+10Q\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=20$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n10Q=20.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=5$ and $Q=2.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡10x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡10x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f2c955db89ff2206ed531f593a149661417e293b1cffba8bae7ce025cdce38d"} {"question" "q-90793" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "