tion/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+40{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +40 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36)-36+40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda -6 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =6\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =6\\pm 2\\text{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation.\n\nThus, any linear combination of the functions $\\text{Re}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and $\\text{Im}({e}^{(6+2\\text{i})x})={e}^{6x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $3{e}^{6x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ is not a solution." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/state.json" "file_sha256" "3f2c955db89ff2206ed531f593a149661417e293b1cffba8bae7ce025cdce38d"} {"question" "q-90807" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0482831521116e3467a76cdb107108638d7d5531b5dd69b53e1c1b2caa227465"} {"question" "q-90807" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "