 our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+25{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+25) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny = P\\cos{5x} + Q\\sin{5x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/example-6595.json" "file_sha256" "7639df0379256c041ecd35cfc2eab070cb9bebdbff0271a8e9f984e9e3bf402a"} {"question" "e-6597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the equation $y'' + 10y' + 34y  = 0,$ where $y = y(t).$ If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/example-6597.json" "file_sha256" "ee4eb6cf25057e30cbbbed2776987475fcd9f42d45ae5385190b16b4d31718cf"} {"question" "e-6597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}+10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+34{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +34) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 34  = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +34 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25)-25+34 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +5)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +5 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-5\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lambda = - 5 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the auxiliary equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/example-6597.json" "file_sha256" "ee4eb6cf25057e30cbbbed2776987475fcd9f42d45ae5385190b16b4d31718cf"} {"question" "e-6598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 4 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 20y =0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/example-6598.json" "file_sha256" "ff58d967ceb0f365447b772384b072b60ce81ac0b5e810336e6b360c98fcccd6"} {"question" "e-6598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x}  = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2}  = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our ODE gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+20{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20 & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +20 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+20 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-16 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 4\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/example-6598.json" "file_sha256" "ff58d967ceb0f365447b772384b072b60ce81ac0b5e810336e6b360c98fcccd6"} {"question" "e-6596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y = y(t)$ satisfies the equation $y'' - 4y' + 85y = 0,$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n- $-2e^{2t}\\cos{9t} + 7e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$\n\n- $e^{2t}\\cos{9t}+3e^{2t}\\sin{3t}$\n\n- $-6e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/example-6596.json" "file_sha256" "0ae92b4cc7b4f0529322af2317a61f4db166457ab7bccaf60943bf0297b43764"} {"question" "e-6596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Assuming $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+85{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 85 = 0,\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +85 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4)-4+85 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2}+81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)^{2} & =-81 \\\\ \\lambda -2 & =\\pm 9\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =2\\pm 9\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\lambda = 2 \\pm 9 \\textrm{i}$ are the complex roots of the characteristic equation, the general solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny = e^{2t} \\left( P \\cos 9t + Q \\sin 9t \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $P=-2$ and $Q=7.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II does *not* take the above form. It contains $\\sin 3t,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III takes the above form with $P=0$ and $Q=-6.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14064047/example-6596.json" "file_sha256" "0ae92b4cc7b4f0529322af2317a61f4db166457ab7bccaf60943bf0297b43764"}] "usage" {"e-6596" [] "e-6598" [] "q-64663" [] "q-64694" [] "e-6595" [] "q-7818" [] "e-6597" [] "q-7816" []} "basis" 2088 "retractions" [[17592186088295 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 16y = 0$."] [17592186088176 :question/problem "Consider the equation $y^{″} - 6y^{′} + 10y = 0$ where $y = y(t)$. If $y = e^{λt}$ is a solution, then $λ =$"] [17592186088177 :question/problem "Consider the equation $y^{″} + 12y^{′} + 40y = 0$, where $y = y(x)$. If $y = e^{λx}$ is a solution, then $λ =$"] [17592186088297 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + 2\\frac{dy}{dx} + 26y = 0$."] [17592186087534 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 25y =0$."] [17592186087534 :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λx} + 25e^{λx} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λx}(λ^{2} + 25) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 25 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 25 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} &= -25 \\\\\nλ &= ± 5\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny = P\\cos{5x} + Q\\sin{5x}\n$$"] [17592186087536 :question/problem "Consider the equation $y'' + 10y' + 34y = 0$, where $y = y(t)$. If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda$?"] [17592186087536 :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny = e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λt} + 10λe^{λt} + 34e^{λt} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λt}(λ^{2} + 10λ + 34) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 10\\lambda + 34 = 0\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 10λ + 34 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 10λ + 25) - 25 + 34 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 5)^{2} + 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 5)^{2} &= -9 \\\\\nλ + 5 &= ± 3\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -5 ± 3\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-5 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nare the complex roots of the auxiliary equation."] [17592186087537 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 4 \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 20y =0$."] [17592186087537 :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our ODE gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λx} - 4λe^{λx} + 20e^{λx} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λx}(λ^{2} - 4λ + 20) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 20 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 20 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 4λ + 4) - 4 + 20 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} + 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} &= -16 \\\\\nλ - 2 &= ± 4\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 2 ± 4\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= e^{2x}(P\\cos 4x + Q\\sin 4x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087535 :question/problem "Given that $y = y(t)$ satisfies the equation $y'' - 4y' + 85y = 0$, which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $-2e^{2t}\\cos{9t} + 7e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$\n2. $e^{2t}\\cos{9t}+3e^{2t}\\sin{3t}$\n3. $-6e^{2t}\\sin{9t}$"] [17592186087535 :question/worked-solution "Assuming\n\n$$\ny = e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda e^{\\lambda t}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λt} - 4λe^{λt} + 85e^{λt} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λt}(λ^{2} - 4λ + 85) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 85 = 0\n$$\n\nand we can solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 4λ + 85 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} - 4λ + 4) - 4 + 85 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} + 81 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 2)^{2} &= -81 \\\\\nλ - 2 &= ± 9\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= 2 ± 9\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2 \\pm 9 \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nare the complex roots of the characteristic equation, the general solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny = e^{2t} \\left(P \\cos 9t + Q \\sin 9t \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $P=-2$ and $Q=7$. So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n- Function II does *not* take the above form. It contains $\\sin 3t$, which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n- Function III takes the above form with $P=0$ and $Q=-6$. So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186085988 17592186085989 17592186085990 17592186085991 17592186085992 17592186085994 17592186085995 17592186085996 17592186085997 17592186085998 17592186086701 17592186086702 17592186086703 17592186086704 17592186086705 17592186086713 17592186086714 17592186086715 17592186086716 17592186086717] "immutable_fields" [[17592186085987 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085987 :answer-field/id] [17592186085987 :answer-field/key] [17592186085987 :answer-field/type] [17592186085993 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085993 :answer-field/id] [17592186085993 :answer-field/key] [17592186085993 :answer-field/type] [17592186086700 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086700 :answer-field/id] [17592186086700 :answer-field/key] [17592186086700 :answer-field/type] [17592186086712 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086712 :answer-field/id] [17592186086712 :answer-field/key] [17592186086712 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
